> How well does Windows 95 or 98 connect to the Unix OS?.

Very well, actually.   Samba was suggested, and it works good.  SCO
also has two products depending on what kind of connectivity you need.

Their first product is called File and Print Services, and after it is
installed and configured, it make the UnixWare (or OpenServer5) look and
act like an NT server, supporting PDCs and BDCs.

SCO's second product is called VisionFS, and it is a simple SMB product
which makes the UnixWare (HP-UX, Solaris, AIX, OpenServer5, etc) look
like a file and print sharing server (no PDC/BDC support or other NT
administration tools).   Bi-directional file and print sharing is
capable from the UW server, and it works quite well.

> My task is to set
> up a LAN so the server receives mail and then sends it to the correct
> terminal.
> How could I do this with Unix and would I need any special software.

POP/IMAP as the mail server software on the UNIX server.  Any POP/IMAP
capable email client can be used on the Win95/98 clients.   Local users
can use Netscape's email reader (from the console running X-windows, or
NC/X-terminals running X-windows), too.

