A /long/ time ago.

> alphas at work (impressed), and so I wondering exactly how "usable" this system
> is. 233mhz sounds fairly decent; however, the system comes with only 24megs

We're talking about EV4's here -- 233MHz isn't /great/, although they
will do just fine for experimenting and whatnot. Don't expect a
233MHz-ish Pentium-like performance.

> of ram. How much can I expect to spend on suitable ram for this unit? Is
> it readily available?

Yup. You're looking for (I believe) 72-pin parity SIMMs; FPM, not EDO.
Consult with http://www.pricewatch.com/ for that. They're not cheap,
though (comparatively; even to new PC1xx DIMMs). You also have to take
into consideration that this is a 64-bit RISC architecture. 24MB of
memory may do you okay for a Linux machine with no X server, but you're
probably going to want at least 64MB for a usable system with X Windows.
I believe Digital^WTru64 UNIX requires you to have 64MB (>>> show mem)
at minimum. You'll find that 96MB will be workable, although with
Netscape running and a few xterms, you'll find yourself swapping more
than you could wish for. I highly recommend you go with 128MB, if you
have the money to throw around.

As for other components, any PC monitor should suffice -- all (most?)
Alphas come with the standard HD15 connector, and is compatible with PC
VGA monitors. And PS/2 mice & keyboards. The 200 should have onboard
10BaseT (maybe even a 10Base2) and so on.

> I am NOT interested in running NT, either linux or maybe true64
> (the non-commercial deal, does with come with access to updates, etc?)

Access to updates? You mean patches? If patches, yes -- you can grab
them from the public anonymous FTP site at
ftp://ftp.service.digital.com/public/Digital_UNIX/ w/o a service
contract. Beware, though, that patchkits can get /large/ (currently, I
belive PK5 for 4.0D is around 85MB).

Hope this info helps.

-y.

