Vladislav Sloboder wrote:
> 
> Dear all,
> 
> I have a problem in my system. When I run my program I get a strange exception message:
> it reads something like this::
> 
> Exception system: exiting due to multiple internal errors
> handler returned invalid disposition
> handler returned invalid disposition
> 
> Please let me know the cause and probable solution

This is from the Digital UNIX exception system (a combined effort of
libc and libexc). It means that an exception was raised, and the
exception system found inconsistent or incorrect data to handle it.

One common cause of this is to raise an exception after process exit has
begun; relatively early in exit(), the exception data is torn down,
guaranteeing that any later exception in the process will fail. This can
happen, for example, if one thread in a multithreaded application calls
exit() (or if main returns), while other thread(s) are about to exit
with pthread_exit, or be cancelled by pthread_cancel (both of which are
implemented using exceptions). It would also happen if a thread threw a
C++ exception.

Another possibility is that the exception data has become corrupted by
some program error -- and if you're not using threads, (or you're using
threads but not C++, cancellation, or thread exit), that's the most
likely explanation.

